{"subjectId":"cpp","id":"perfect-forwarding","title":"Forwarding References & std::forward","group":"Templates & Generic Programming","order":45,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A template parameter <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> is a <em>forwarding reference</em>: it binds to lvalues and rvalues alike. <code>std::forward&lt;T&gt;</code> passes the argument on with its original value category, so moves stay moves and copies stay copies.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-minimize\"></i>Reference collapsing</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Argument</th><th><code>T</code> deduced as</th><th><code>T&amp;&amp;</code> becomes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>lvalue <code>int x</code></td><td><code>int&amp;</code></td><td><code>int&amp; &amp;&amp;</code> → <code>int&amp;</code></td></tr><tr><td>rvalue <code>42</code> / <code>std::move(x)</code></td><td><code>int</code></td><td><code>int&amp;&amp;</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Rule: <code>&amp; &amp;</code>, <code>&amp; &amp;&amp;</code> and <code>&amp;&amp; &amp;</code> collapse to <code>&amp;</code>; only <code>&amp;&amp; &amp;&amp;</code> stays <code>&amp;&amp;</code>. A <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> is a forwarding reference <strong>only</strong> when <code>T</code> is being deduced (a template parameter or <code>auto&amp;&amp;</code>) — <code>std::string&amp;&amp;</code> is a plain rvalue reference.</p>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-question-mark\"></i>The problem <code>std::forward</code> solves</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\n\nvoid consume(const std::string&amp;) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy\\n\"; }\nvoid consume(std::string&amp;&amp;)      { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"move\\n\"; }\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nvoid wrapperBad(T&amp;&amp; arg) { consume(arg); }                    // a named variable is an lvalue!\n\ntemplate &lt;typename T&gt;\nvoid wrapperGood(T&amp;&amp; arg) { consume(std::forward&lt;T&gt;(arg)); }  // restores the category\n\nint main() {\n    std::string s = \"x\";\n    wrapperBad(s);              wrapperBad(std::string(\"t\"));\n    wrapperGood(s);             wrapperGood(std::string(\"t\"));\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>copy\ncopy\ncopy\nmove</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th></th><th><code>std::move(x)</code></th><th><code>std::forward&lt;T&gt;(x)</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What it does</td><td>Unconditionally casts to rvalue</td><td>Casts to rvalue <em>only if</em> the original argument was an rvalue</td></tr><tr><td>Use with</td><td>Objects you own and are done with</td><td>Forwarding references <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> in templates</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-push\"></i>Real use: emplace</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;string&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\n\nstruct Order {\n    std::string item;\n    int qty;\n    Order(std::string i, int q) : item(std::move(i)), qty(q) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"construct\\n\"; }\n    Order(const Order&amp; o) : item(o.item), qty(o.qty) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"copy\\n\"; }\n    Order(Order&amp;&amp; o) noexcept : item(std::move(o.item)), qty(o.qty) { std::cout &lt;&lt; \"move\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    std::vector&lt;Order&gt; orders;\n    orders.reserve(4);\n    orders.push_back(Order(\"pen\", 2));     // construct a temporary, then move it in\n    orders.emplace_back(\"ink\", 1);         // forwards the arguments: construct in place\n}</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>construct\nmove\nconstruct</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Don't <code>std::forward</code> the same argument twice — the first call may already have moved from it. And don't overload a function on a forwarding reference: <code>template &lt;class T&gt; void f(T&amp;&amp;)</code> is an exact match for almost everything and will hijack your other overloads.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is perfect forwarding?</summary><div><p>Passing arguments through a generic wrapper to another function exactly as they were given — preserving lvalue/rvalue-ness and const — using a forwarding reference <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> plus <code>std::forward&lt;T&gt;(arg)</code>. It's how <code>make_unique</code>, <code>emplace_back</code> and <code>std::thread</code>'s constructor avoid unnecessary copies.</p></div></details></section>"}